{"name":"Boogie Blast","hex":"#119944","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"boogie","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A piece of solid or semisolid mucus in or removed from the nostril cavity."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A style of swing dance.","Any relatively energetic dance to pop or rock music."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/boogie"},{"term":"blast","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A violent gust of wind (in windy weather) or apparent wind (around a moving vehicle).","A forcible stream of gas or liquid from an orifice, for example from a bellows, the tuyeres of a blast furnace, a person's mouth, etc."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make an impression on, by making a loud blast or din.","To make a loud noise."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blast"}],"entries":[{"title":"Boogie","blocks":[{"text":"Boogie is a repetitive, swung note or shuffle rhythm, \"groove\" or pattern used in blues which was originally played on the piano in boogie-woogie music. The characteristic rhythm and feel of the boogie was then adapted to guitar, double bass, and other instruments. The earliest recorded boogie-woogie song was in 1916. By the 1930s, swing bands such as Benny Goodman, Glenn Miller, Tommy Dorsey and Louis Jordan all had boogie hits. By the 1950s, boogie became incorporated into the emerging rockabilly and rock and roll styles. In the late 1980s and the early 1990s, country bands released country boogies. Today, the term \"boogie\" usually refers to dancing to pop, disco, or rock music."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boogie","wikidata":"Q3263753"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Blues_shuffle_in_E.png/500px-Blues_shuffle_in_E.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Blues_shuffle_in_E.png/500px-Blues_shuffle_in_E.png?utm_sou